<p dir=3D"auto">For 10 items of 100ms each, which is faster?</p>
<ul dir=3D"auto">
<li>A) Both take ~100ms =E2=80=94 JS is parallel</li>
<li>B) <code>processAll</code> takes ~1000ms; <code>processAllFast</code> t=
akes ~100ms</li>
<li>C) <code>processAll</code> takes ~100ms; <code>processAllFast</code> ca=
uses race conditions</li>
<li>D) Both take ~1000ms =E2=80=94 JavaScript is single-threaded</li>
</ul>
<details>
<summary><strong>Answer &amp; Explanation</strong></summary>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Answer: B) <code>processAll</code> takes ~1000ms; <=
code>processAllFast</code> takes ~100ms</strong></p>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Explanation:</strong> <code>await</code> in a loop =
processes items <strong>sequentially</strong> (one after another) =E2=80=94=
 10 =C3=83=E2=80=94 100ms =3D ~1000ms. <code>Promise.all</code> starts <str=
ong>all</strong> async operations simultaneously and resolves when all comp=
lete =E2=80=94 ~100ms. Use <code>Promise.all</code> for independent operati=
ons. Sequential <code>await</code> is only needed when each operation depen=
ds on the previous result.</p>
</details>
