t access <code>this</code></li>
<li>D) <code>"I am Rex"</code> =E2=80=94 <code>super.describe()</code> stop=
s at Animal</li>
</ul>
<details>
<summary><strong>Answer &amp; Explanation</strong></summary>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Answer: A) <code>"I am Rex, a Labrador"</code></str=
ong></p>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Explanation:</strong> <code>super.describe()</code>=
 calls the parent class method, but <code>this</code> inside that parent me=
thod still refers to the <strong>current instance</strong> (<code>d</code>)=
. So <code>this.name</code> in <code>Animal.describe()</code> resolves to <=
code>'Rex'</code> from the <code>Dog</code> instance. <code>super</code> de=
termines which method to call, not which <code>this</code> to use.</p>
</details>
