<p dir=3D"auto">What does <code>concurrency =3D 3</code> provide?</p>
<ul dir=3D"auto">
<li>A) Sequential processing =E2=80=94 only 1 task at a time</li>
<li>B) A maximum of 3 concurrent async tasks, preventing resource exhaustio=
n while allowing parallelism</li>
<li>C) The queue processes exactly 3 tasks total and then stops</li>
<li>D) Tasks are processed in reverse order</li>
</ul>
<details>
<summary><strong>Answer &amp; Explanation</strong></summary>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Answer: B) A maximum of 3 concurrent async tasks, p=
reventing resource exhaustion</strong></p>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Explanation:</strong> Without concurrency control, =
flooding a server with 100 simultaneous requests could cause rate limiting =
or resource exhaustion. The queue runs up to <code>concurrency</code> (3) t=
asks simultaneously. When one completes, the next queued task starts (<code=
>#runNext</code>). This pattern is essential for: bulk API operations, file=
 processing, database migrations, and any scenario where parallelism must b=
e bounded.</p>
</details>
