<ul dir=3D"auto">
<li>A) Option A =E2=80=94 inline try/catch is more explicit and easier for =
junior developers to understand</li>
<li>B) Option B =E2=80=94 centralized error middleware ensures consistent e=
rror responses, logging, and status codes across all routes without duplica=
ting error-handling logic</li>
<li>C) Both are equivalent; the choice is purely stylistic</li>
<li>D) Option B is dangerous because unhandled errors in <code>asyncHandler=
</code> will crash the server</li>
</ul>
<details>
<summary><strong>Answer &amp; Explanation</strong></summary>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Answer: B) Option B =E2=80=94 centralized error mid=
dleware ensures consistent error responses, logging, and status codes acros=
s all routes without duplicating error-handling logic</strong></p>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Explanation:</strong> Option A scatters error handl=
ing across every route, leading to inconsistent formats, missing logging, a=
nd high maintenance overhead. A Tech Lead should implement a central error =
middleware that all routes funnel into via <code>next(err)</code>, combined=
 with a typed error hierarchy (<code>AppError</code>, <code>ValidationError=
</code>, etc.) to produce structured, consistent API error responses with p=
roper HTTP status codes.</p>
</details>
