<li>A) This pattern is inferior to throwing exceptions in all cases</li>
<li>B) The Result type makes errors explicit in the function signature =E2=
=80=94 callers must handle the error case, preventing forgotten error handl=
ing</li>
<li>C) This pattern prevents TypeScript from working correctly</li>
<li>D) The <code>ok</code> flag should be removed for simplicity</li>
</ul>
<details>
<summary><strong>Answer &amp; Explanation</strong></summary>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Answer: B) Result type makes errors explicit =E2=80=
=94 callers must handle both success and failure</strong></p>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Explanation:</strong> With exceptions, callers can =
forget to <code>try/catch</code>. The Result type forces callers to check <=
code>result.ok</code> before using <code>result.value</code>. This pattern =
(from Rust's <code>Result&lt;T, E&gt;</code>, Haskell's <code>Either</code>=
) makes the error possibility part of the API contract. TypeScript can type=
 this as <code>{ ok: true; value: T } | { ok: false; error: string }</code>=
 with full type narrowing. Useful for predictable failure cases (validation=
, parsing, business rules).</p>
</details>
